The elitist believes some people are naturally superior and that society should keep those hierarchies. Of the listed heads, Pride (878) and Insolence (885) cover haughtiness and arrogance, which are attitudes rather than a belief in hierarchy. Nobility (875) is about rank itself, and Superiority (33) is abstract and general. None of them names a belief in the natural superiority of certain groups, though Pride's "lordly" and "haughtiness" come closest to the elitist attitude.

Pride is only a loose fit, because the elitist's feature is a belief in hierarchy rather than personal pride. Nobility describes the rank itself, not someone who upholds the system. So I judge there is no true home here.

